{#
  Migration 0102 — THE DISPOSITION IS TYPED BY THE RECORD, NOT BY THE SIGNER.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 3 · §2.2 findings S1, S7, S8, S19 · §16 MI11, MI27, MI28, MI29 ·
  docs/leads/kernel.md rulings D2, D3, D4 · spec/binding/authority-source.md §5.

  This is the second half of finding S1 and the whole of finding S7. Five separate things a
  writer could otherwise assert about itself become projections here:

    virulence / closure_gen / severity_snapshot   RE-DERIVED FROM THE CLOSURE, at this
        transaction's timestamp, THROUGH the check's (clause_uuid, commit_id) — never inherited
        from `blocking_check`. That is not belt-and-braces. `blocking_check.virulence` is itself
        a projection, and a projection of a projection is a copy: if the closure moved between
        the check landing and the signature, the disposition must be typed by the closure the
        signer was actually exposed to, not by a stale scalar. Re-deriving is also what makes a
        laundered check unable to launder its disposition even if 0100 were unwelded.

    signer_rank / signer_org / competency triple  PROJECTED FROM THE LIVE `person` ROW AND
        FROZEN. Finding S7: before this, rank, organisation and competency were attacker-supplied
        strings, so `rank_floor`, `needs_second_signer` and `needs_foreign_org` were comparing
        the writer's own assertions with each other. A client signing with signer_rank = 6 on a
        person whose live rank is 2 stores 2, and `rank_floor` then returns 23514 with its name
        attached. That is conformance case CF-19.

        FROZEN, not joined. A live join to the HR system returns TODAY's competency at trial,
        which is useless and looks like backfilling. The frozen snapshot supports a provable
        claim: at this instant the system checked, the record said this, and here is the digest
        of the source record.

    deliberation_seconds   COMPUTED FROM THE SERVER-ISSUED `exposure_receipt.issued_at`, a
        DEFAULT now() on a row the kernel wrote. `presented_at` — a client-supplied timestamp —
        was deleted for this reason. A signer cannot claim to have read for longer than the
        server showed them the material.

    prior_override_count   COUNTED FROM `override_ledger` ACROSS PERMITS for this person at this
        site (finding S8). The override ladder escalates against the PERSON with no ceiling, so
        `override_escalates` compares a rank the record projected against a count the record
        kept. A per-permit counter was a bypass that reset itself.

  RULING D3 — A MISSING CLEARANCE ROW DOES NOT RAISE A SYNTHETIC 23503.
  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 raises `23503` from the trigger when the clearance lookup misses. That is
  wrong twice: a synthetic 23503 carries NO CONSTRAINT NAME, and the constraint name is the
  exhibit (spec/errors.md §3.3 bans it outright); and leaving the NOT NULL requirement columns
  unset would emit 23502, which is outside the refusal taxonomy entirely. So the miss projects
  the STRICTEST legal values and returns the row, and the real composite foreign key
  `fk_clearance` fires 23503 WITH ITS NAME. The strictest values are themselves refusing, so if
  that foreign key were ever dropped the row would still be refused by `rank_floor`,
  `needs_second_signer`, `needs_foreign_org`, `needs_predicate` and `needs_reassert` — a refusal
  depth of six from one declaration.

  RULING D4 — NO `CASE` EXPRESSION ANYWHERE IN THIS BODY. `GT-13` has not answered whether a
  `CASE` expression is legal inside a PL/pgSQL statement on this platform, and PL-3 forbids
  resting a dated path on an unproven capability. Boolean casts are unconditionally legal and
  are used instead.

  THE ONE AGGREGATE. §4.1 law 4 allows one aggregate `SELECT … INTO` per body. Two counts are
  needed — verbatim anchors and prior overrides — so they are two scalar subqueries inside ONE
  `SELECT … INTO`, measured working on v26.2.5. That is the letter and the spirit: one aggregate
  statement, no loop, no `RECORD`, no `PERFORM`, no `EXECUTE`, no `GET DIAGNOSTICS`.

  WHAT THIS FUNCTION DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO.
    * It does not raise for a disposition whose kind is illegal at this virulence. The composite
      foreign key does, with its name (spec/errors.md §3.3 corollary).
    * It does not threshold `reading_floor_met`. Breaching the reading-rate floor is RECORDED and
      PRICED — it projects onto the subject's unmet-floor counter and the merge then needs a
      countersignature from a differently-credentialed second signer. Fast stays legal; it just
      names a second person (finding S19, invariant I15).
    * It does not name a human's competence, honesty or attentiveness in any message. Messages
      name rows and rules (spec/errors.md §3.2, invariant I15).

  A MEASURED ORDERING CONSEQUENCE NOBODY SHOULD DISCOVER IN PRODUCTION.
  `verbatim_anchor_count` is counted from `disposition_citation`, whose `disposition_id` carries a
  foreign key TO `disposition`. A citation therefore cannot exist before the row it cites, so at
  BEFORE INSERT this count is NECESSARILY ZERO, and `verbatim_floor` — which admits a
  `mechanism_absent` or `mitigated` disposition only when the anchor count reaches
  `required_anchors` — is satisfiable today only while `required_anchors` is its default of zero.
  That is fail-CLOSED and therefore safe, and it is also a live limit on the mechanism: the moment
  anything projects a non-zero `required_anchors`, those two disposition kinds become
  unreachable. Closing it needs a decision this file cannot make — a staging relation the writer
  fills before the disposition, or dropping the citation table's foreign key — and it belongs to
  whoever owns `disposition_citation`. It is recorded here rather than discovered later.

  DECLARED VERSUS UNDECLARED PROJECTIONS — READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A COLUMN.
  Nine of the columns written here are backed by `[[authority_source]]` entries in the binding and
  are named in the `@projects` pragmas above; the Authority Source Contract enforces them at
  render time. The rest — `subject_kind`, the two subject-id columns, `site_id`, `virulence`,
  `closure_gen`, `severity_snapshot`, `competency_source_id`, `competency_snapshot`,
  `max_ttl_hours`, `prior_override_count`, `verbatim_anchor_count`, `deliberation_seconds`,
  `reading_floor_met` and the countersigner identity pair — are projected from the same three
  declared relations but are NOT listed in any entry's `projects`, so the contract does not see
  them. That is a gap in the BINDING, not in this file: `vertical.toml` is owned by the render
  worker, and rule A-1 refuses a pragma naming a column no entry backs, so this template cannot
  close it unilaterally. It is recorded here, in the rendered SQL, where a reviewer reads it.

  PLATFORM (measured on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, local single node, 2026-08-09):
    * `(NEW).col` for every read — the unparenthesised spelling creates and then fails at
      execution with 42P01;
    * a `DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()` primary key IS populated before a BEFORE INSERT trigger runs,
      so `(NEW).disposition_id` is a real value here and the citation count addresses a real row;
    * two scalar subqueries inside one `SELECT … INTO` are accepted.
#}
{% set strictest = {
     'req_compensating':  'true',
     'req_second_signer': 'true',
     'req_foreign_org':   'true',
     'req_predicate':     'true',
     'req_reassert':      'true',
     'min_signer_rank':   '9',
   } %}
{% set closurekeytype = {
     'clause_uuid': 'UUID',
     'commit_id':   'BYTES',
   } %}
{% set vartype = {
     'req_compensating':  'BOOL',
     'req_second_signer': 'BOOL',
     'req_foreign_org':   'BOOL',
     'req_predicate':     'BOOL',
     'req_reassert':      'BOOL',
     'min_signer_rank':   'INT2',
     'signer_rank':       'INT2',
     'signer_org':        'STRING',
     'competency_sha256': 'BYTES',
   } %}
{% set ns = namespace(closure=none, clearance=none, person=none) %}
{% for a in authority_sources %}
{%   if 'blocking_check.severity' in a.projects %}{% set ns.closure = a %}{% endif %}
{%   if 'disposition.min_signer_rank' in a.projects %}{% set ns.clearance = a %}{% endif %}
{%   if 'disposition.signer_rank' in a.projects %}{% set ns.person = a %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if ns.closure is none %}{{ binding_declares_no_authority_source_for_blocking_check_severity }}{% endif %}
{% if ns.clearance is none %}{{ binding_declares_no_authority_source_for_disposition_min_signer_rank }}{% endif %}
{% if ns.person is none %}{{ binding_declares_no_authority_source_for_disposition_signer_rank }}{% endif %}
{% set cl = ns.clearance %}
{% set pe = ns.person %}
{% set clnames = [] %}{% for p in cl.projects %}{% set _ = clnames.append(p.split('.')[1]) %}{% endfor %}
{% set penames = [] %}{% for p in pe.projects %}{% set _ = penames.append(p.split('.')[1]) %}{% endfor %}
{% set clreads = [] %}{% for c in cl.columns %}{% set _ = clreads.append('cl.' ~ c) %}{% endfor %}
{% set clintos = [] %}{% for n in clnames %}{% set _ = clintos.append('v_' ~ n) %}{% endfor %}
{% set pereads = [] %}{% for c in pe.columns %}{% set _ = pereads.append('pr.' ~ c) %}{% endfor %}
{% set peintos = [] %}{% for n in penames %}{% set _ = peintos.append('v_' ~ n) %}{% endfor %}
{% set copreds = [] %}{% for kc in pe.key_columns %}{% set _ = copreds.append('pr.' ~ kc ~ ' = (NEW).countersigner_sub') %}{% endfor %}
{% set pepreds = [] %}{% for kc in pe.key_columns %}{% set _ = pepreds.append('pr.' ~ kc ~ ' = (NEW).' ~ pe.key[loop.index0]) %}{% endfor %}
{% set cspreds = [] %}{% for kc in ns.closure.key_columns %}{% set _ = cspreds.append('c.' ~ kc ~ ' = v_ck_' ~ ns.closure.key[loop.index0]) %}{% endfor %}
{% set ckreads = [] %}{% for k in ns.closure.key %}{% set _ = ckreads.append('bc.' ~ k) %}{% endfor %}
{% set ckintos = [] %}{% for k in ns.closure.key %}{% set _ = ckintos.append('v_ck_' ~ k) %}{% endfor %}
{% set bcreads = [] %}{% for s in subjects %}{% set _ = bcreads.append('bc.' ~ s.id_column) %}{% endfor %}
{% set bcintos = [] %}{% for s in subjects %}{% set _ = bcintos.append('v_' ~ s.id_column) %}{% endfor %}
-- @file 0102_fn_disposition_project.sql
{{ header(file='0102_fn_disposition_project.sql',
          title='CREATE FUNCTION ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.fn_disposition_project — identity, virulence and deliberation are projections',
          rationale='Findings S1 and S7 in one function. Virulence, closure generation and severity are re-derived from the blame closure rather than inherited from the blocking check, so a laundered check cannot launder its disposition. Rank, organisation and the competency triple are projected from the live person row and frozen, so a client signing with a rank it does not hold is overwritten before rank_floor compares. Deliberation derives from the server-issued exposure receipt. A missing clearance row projects the strictest legal values under ruling D3 rather than raising a synthetic 23503, because a synthetic code carries no constraint name and the constraint name is the exhibit.',
          mi=['MI11', 'MI27', 'MI28', 'MI29'], i=['I09', 'I10', 'I15']) }}
--
-- migration:  0102_fn_disposition_project
-- band:       0100-0109z · kernel/projection-triggers · RENDERED
-- statements: 1
-- invariants: MI27 (identity, rank, org and competency are projections of person; a missing
--             person row refuses) · MI11 (no disposition kind dismisses a fatality-written
--             control — enforced by fk_clearance, ARMED by the virulence projected here) ·
--             MI29 (overrides escalate against the person across permits, no ceiling)
-- source:     ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 3 · §2.2 S1, S7, S8, S19 ·
--             docs/leads/kernel.md D2, D3, D4 · spec/binding/authority-source.md §5
-- requires:   {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check (0058) · {{ binding.schema }}.exposure_receipt (0061) ·
--             {{ binding.schema }}.disposition_citation (0067) · {{ binding.schema }}.override_ledger (0068) ·
--             {{ ns.closure.relation }} · {{ cl.relation }} · {{ pe.relation }} ·
--             {{ zone.meas }}.recall_policy (the reading-rate policy; absent values fall back to
--             the substrate defaults tau0 = 5 s and rho = 4 tokens/s, which is a DEGRADATION,
--             not a pass — the floor still computes and still projects)
-- provides:   {{ binding.schema }}.fn_disposition_project() — welded to disposition by 0122
-- sqlstate:   P0001 on an unknown check, an absent closure, an absent or expired exposure
--             receipt, and an unknown signer or countersigner. 23503 on fk_clearance for an
--             illegal (virulence, kind) pair — raised BY THE FOREIGN KEY, never here (D3).
-- forward-only; no .down.sql and no .up.sql (MR-5).
--
-- @projects {{ cl.projects | join(', ') }}
-- @authority {{ cl.relation }} ({{ cl.key_columns | join(', ') }}) <= NEW ({{ cl.key | join(', ') }})
-- @on_missing raise
--
-- @projects {{ pe.projects | join(', ') }}
-- @authority {{ pe.relation }} ({{ pe.key_columns | join(', ') }}) <= NEW ({{ pe.key | join(', ') }})
-- @on_missing raise
--
-- ACYCLICITY. BEFORE INSERT on disposition, writing only to NEW. It reads six relations and
-- writes none of them. Trigger depth contributed: 0.

CREATE FUNCTION {{ binding.schema }}.fn_disposition_project() RETURNS TRIGGER LANGUAGE PLpgSQL AS $$
DECLARE
  v_subject_kind STRING;
{% for s in subjects %}
  v_{{ s.id_column }} UUID;
{% endfor %}
  v_site_id UUID;
{% for k in ns.closure.key %}
  v_ck_{{ k }} {{ closurekeytype[k] }};
{% endfor %}
  v_severity INT2;
  v_virulence {{ binding.schema }}.virulence_class;
  v_closure_gen INT8;
{% for n in clnames %}
  v_{{ n }} {{ vartype[n] }};
{% endfor %}
  v_max_ttl_hours INT4;
{% for n in penames %}
  v_{{ n }} {{ vartype[n] }};
{% endfor %}
  v_competency_source_id UUID;
  v_competency_snapshot JSONB;
  v_co_rank INT2;
  v_co_org STRING;
  v_issued_at TIMESTAMPTZ;
  v_expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ;
  v_tokens INT8;
  v_tau0 FLOAT8;
  v_rho FLOAT8;
  v_anchors INT4;
  v_overrides INT8;
  v_deliberation INT8;
BEGIN
  -- 1 · WHICH OBLIGATION IS THIS. The subject, the site and the clause version all come from the
  -- check row, never from the inserter: a disposition that names its own permit is a disposition
  -- that can be filed against a permit its check never blocked.
  SELECT bc.subject_kind, {{ bcreads | join(', ') }}, bc.site_id, {{ ckreads | join(', ') }}
    INTO v_subject_kind, {{ bcintos | join(', ') }}, v_site_id, {{ ckintos | join(', ') }}
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check bc
   WHERE bc.check_id = (NEW).check_id;
  IF v_subject_kind IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE='P0001',
      MESSAGE='{{ binding.name }}: no such blocking check — a disposition cannot be filed against an obligation that does not exist';
  END IF;

  -- 2 · FINDING S1. Virulence comes from the CLOSURE at this transaction's timestamp, reached
  -- through the check's clause version. It is never inherited from the check row, so a laundered
  -- check cannot launder the disposition even if 0100 were disabled.
  SELECT c.{{ ns.closure.columns[0] }}, c.{{ ns.closure.columns[1] }}, c.{{ ns.closure.columns[2] }}
    INTO v_severity, v_virulence, v_closure_gen
    FROM {{ ns.closure.relation }} c
   WHERE {{ cspreds | join(' AND ') }};
  IF v_severity IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE='P0001',
      MESSAGE='{{ binding.name }}: blame closure absent — a disposition cannot be typed';
  END IF;

  -- 3 · WHAT WAS ACTUALLY SHOWN, AND WHEN THE SERVER SHOWED IT. `issued_at` is a server DEFAULT
  -- now() on a row the kernel wrote. Expiry is a trigger comparison and never a CHECK, because
  -- now() is not immutable and a CHECK sees only the row being written (§4.1 law 1).
  SELECT er.issued_at, er.expires_at, er.total_tokens
    INTO v_issued_at, v_expires_at, v_tokens
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.exposure_receipt er
   WHERE er.receipt_id = (NEW).receipt_id;
  IF v_expires_at IS NULL OR v_expires_at <= now() THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE='P0001',
      MESSAGE='{{ binding.name }}: exposure receipt absent or expired — re-materialise before signing';
  END IF;

  -- 4 · THE CLEARANCE LATTICE, keyed on the virulence THIS FUNCTION derived and the kind the
  -- signer chose. Ruling D3: a miss projects the strictest values and returns; fk_clearance then
  -- refuses with 23503 and its own name.
  -- The first key column is `{{ cl.key[0] }}`, and it is READ FROM v_virulence RATHER THAN FROM
  -- NEW. That substitution IS finding S1: reading (NEW).{{ cl.key[0] }} here would let the signer
  -- choose which row of the clearance lattice judges the signature.
  SELECT {{ clreads | join(', ') }}, cl.max_ttl_hours
    INTO {{ clintos | join(', ') }}, v_max_ttl_hours
    FROM {{ cl.relation }} cl
   WHERE cl.{{ cl.key_columns[0] }} = v_virulence
     AND cl.{{ cl.key_columns[1] }} = (NEW).{{ cl.key[1] }};
  IF v_{{ clnames[0] }} IS NULL THEN
{% for n in clnames %}
    v_{{ n }} := {{ strictest[n] }};
{% endfor %}
    v_max_ttl_hours := NULL;
  END IF;

  -- 5 · FINDING S7. Identity and competency are projected from the live person row and frozen
  -- here. The current row for a signer is the FIRST row of the primary-key scan, because
  -- `person` is keyed (signer_sub, effective_from DESC).
  SELECT {{ pereads | join(', ') }}, pr.competency_source_id, pr.competency_snapshot
    INTO {{ peintos | join(', ') }}, v_competency_source_id, v_competency_snapshot
    FROM {{ pe.relation }} pr
   WHERE {{ pepreds | join(' AND ') }}
     AND pr.effective_from <= now()
   ORDER BY pr.effective_from DESC
   LIMIT 1;
  IF v_{{ penames[0] }} IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE='P0001',
      MESSAGE='{{ binding.name }}: no competency record for this signer — fail closed';
  END IF;

  -- 6 · THE COUNTERSIGNER IS A SIGNER. `needs_foreign_org` compares two organisation strings; if
  -- one of them is supplied by the party the requirement constrains, the requirement is a
  -- formality. Projected from the same relation, or NULL when there is no countersigner.
  IF (NEW).countersigner_sub IS NULL THEN
    v_co_rank := NULL;
    v_co_org := NULL;
  ELSE
    SELECT pr.{{ pe.columns[0] }}, pr.{{ pe.columns[1] }}
      INTO v_co_rank, v_co_org
      FROM {{ pe.relation }} pr
     WHERE {{ copreds | join(' AND ') }}
       AND pr.effective_from <= now()
     ORDER BY pr.effective_from DESC
     LIMIT 1;
    IF v_co_rank IS NULL THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE='P0001',
        MESSAGE='{{ binding.name }}: no competency record for this countersigner — fail closed';
    END IF;
  END IF;

  -- 7 · THE ONE AGGREGATE STATEMENT (§4.1 law 4). Verbatim anchors bound what may ACQUIT; the
  -- override count is the ladder that escalates against the person across permits (S8).
  SELECT (SELECT count(*)
            FROM {{ binding.schema }}.disposition_citation dc
           WHERE dc.disposition_id = (NEW).disposition_id
             AND dc.kind = 'verbatim')::INT4,
         (SELECT count(*)
            FROM {{ binding.schema }}.override_ledger ol
           WHERE ol.site_id = v_site_id
             AND ol.signer_sub = (NEW).signer_sub)::INT8
    INTO v_anchors, v_overrides;

  -- 8 · The reading-rate policy. Absent values degrade to the substrate defaults rather than
  -- refusing, because the floor is a MEASUREMENT that prices a countersignature, not a gate.
  SELECT (rp.tau->>'tau0')::FLOAT8, (rp.tau->>'rho')::FLOAT8
    INTO v_tau0, v_rho
    FROM {{ zone.meas }}.recall_policy rp
    JOIN {{ binding.schema }}.exposure_receipt er2 ON er2.policy_version = rp.policy_version
   WHERE er2.receipt_id = (NEW).receipt_id;

  -- 9 · THE PROJECTION. Unconditional, every one of them.
  NEW.subject_kind := v_subject_kind;
{% for s in subjects %}
  NEW.{{ s.id_column }} := v_{{ s.id_column }};
{% endfor %}
  NEW.site_id := v_site_id;
  NEW.virulence := v_virulence;
  NEW.closure_gen := v_closure_gen;
  NEW.severity_snapshot := v_severity;
{% for n in clnames %}
  NEW.{{ n }} := v_{{ n }};
{% endfor %}
  NEW.max_ttl_hours := v_max_ttl_hours;
{% for n in penames %}
  NEW.{{ n }} := v_{{ n }};
{% endfor %}
  NEW.competency_source_id := v_competency_source_id;
  NEW.competency_snapshot := v_competency_snapshot;
  NEW.countersigner_rank := v_co_rank;
  NEW.countersigner_org := v_co_org;
  NEW.verbatim_anchor_count := v_anchors;
  NEW.prior_override_count := v_overrides;

  -- 10 · DELIBERATION AND THE READING-RATE FLOOR (S19). t_min = tau0 + tokens / rho, with rho
  -- deliberately generous at 4 tokens/s (about 240 words per minute). Positive polarity: the
  -- column records that the floor WAS met, so a row that never reached this function reads as
  -- unmet rather than as compliant.
  v_deliberation := extract(epoch FROM (now() - v_issued_at))::INT8;
  NEW.deliberation_seconds := v_deliberation;
  NEW.reading_floor_met :=
    (v_deliberation::FLOAT8 >= coalesce(v_tau0, 5::FLOAT8)
                             + (v_tokens::FLOAT8 / coalesce(v_rho, 4::FLOAT8)));
  RETURN NEW;
END $$;
